Universal Embodiment Architecture (UEA), v0.4

A Falsifiable Theory of Capability-Mediated Transfer Across Heterogeneous Robotic Bodies

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM


Abstract

Cross-embodiment learning transfers policies, demonstrations or skills between robot bodies, but it lacks a predictive theory: an estimate, before transfer, of the adaptation effort required, indexed by learner and task. We propose the Universal Embodiment Architecture (UEA), a framework whose central formal object is a task-conditioned, distributional capability profile built from scalar marginals, together with a learner-indexed transfer-cost model whose primary endpoint is the right-censored sample complexity N^\star to a pre-registered success threshold.

The theoretical contribution is a conditional bridge. We prove that if a transition-sensitive capability marginal exists — one whose performance functional is Lipschitz in the one-step predictive distribution — then the directional capability distance controls the visited-distribution dynamics mismatch that governs zero-shot value loss (Theorem 3). We then give an operational selection rule for that marginal (Theorem 3′), a Bayes-risk lower bound showing that morphology-only predictors cannot succeed on the discordant cell by construction (Theorem 4), a probe-economy certificate derived from the optimal-transport curse of dimensionality (Corollary 5), and a feasibility-margin stability lemma making the runtime-assurance calibration cost a predictable function of dynamics distance (Lemma 6). The framework is pre-registered, powered, and equipped with kill criteria that guarantee the experiment detects separation failure if it occurs.

Keywords: cross-embodiment transfer, capability representation, censored survival analysis, control barrier functions, pre-registration.

---

1. Introduction

The humanoid form is an infrastructural default, not a scientific necessity. If an artificial cognitive system is to operate across humanoid, legged, wheeled, aerial and aquatic bodies, the engineering question is not whether transfer is possible — cross-embodiment systems already transfer policies and demonstrations  — but how much must be re-learned when the body changes, and whether that quantity can be predicted before the transfer is performed.

Let I denote a cognitive architecture, B_i,B_j two embodiments, \mathcal E an environment family, \tau a task, and \mathcal L a learning method. UEA seeks a function
\widehat{TC}_{i\to j}=G\big(\mathbf d_i,\mathbf d_j \mid \mathcal L,\tau\big), \tag{1}


where \mathbf d_i is a description of body i obtainable without performing the transfer. The scientific question is which descriptions make (1) accurate, and how much predictive information resides in capability descriptions versus morphological ones. The framework does not claim to invent cross-embodiment learning, universal controllers, robot ontologies or runtime assurance; it claims that capability descriptions, if they control the dynamics mismatch on the visited distribution, predict transfer cost better than morphology, and it specifies what would falsify that claim.

