2. Measure-Theoretic Foundations and Piecewise-Smooth Dynamics

2.1 Assumption set

A0 (Polish structure). For every embodiment i, the spaces \mathcal X_i,\mathcal U_i,\mathcal Z_i are Polish with their Borel \sigma-algebras; the environment family (\mathcal E,\mathcal B(\mathcal E),\mu_{\mathcal E}) is a standard Borel probability space.

A1 (Borel kernels). The transition kernel F_i:\mathcal X_i\times\mathcal U_i\times\mathcal E\to\mathcal P(\mathcal X_i) is a Borel-measurable Markov kernel, and likewise the sensing kernel H_i. Under A0–A1, the Ionescu–Tulcea theorem guarantees the existence and measurability of the closed-loop law P_{i,e,\pi}.

A2 (bounded, measurable functionals). Rewards r_\tau and capability functionals \varphi_k are bounded and measurable, so every scalar marginal P^\tau_{ik} has finite moments of all orders.

A3 (piecewise-Lipschitz transitions). There is a closed, measure-zero contact set \mathcal S_i\subset\mathcal X_i\times\mathcal U_i such that F_i is locally Lipschitz in total variation on \mathcal X_i\setminus\mathcal S_i, with Lipschitz constant L_F, and has a bounded jump across \mathcal S_i. Systems with vector fields that are \mathbb C^2 except on a closed measure-zero subset and Lipschitz elsewhere are the standard object of piecewise-smooth invariance analysis . This replaces the unrealistic global Lipschitz axiom of earlier drafts: legged contact dynamics are discontinuous at switching, and the assumption must admit them.

A4 (finite entropic moment). For each scalar marginal, J(P)=\int_0^1\sqrt{F^{-1}(q)(1-F^{-1}(q))}\,dq<\infty, equivalently \mathbb E_P[X^2]<\infty.

A5 (feasibility margin). For the runtime-assurance layer, \mu_i>0 as defined in (16) below.

2.2 The measure-zero contact argument

Under A3, the mismatch functional
\delta_{ij}(\rho_\pi)=\mathbb E_{x\sim\rho_\pi}\Big[\mathrm{TV}\big(F_i(\cdot\mid x,\pi(x)),\ \psi_{ij}^*F_j(\cdot\mid \psi_{ij}x,\psi_{ij}\pi(x))\big)\Big]


is well defined provided the state-occupancy measure \rho_\pi places zero mass on \mathcal S_i. This is an assumption on the probe distribution, not a universal fact: if \rho_{\mathrm{probe}} concentrates on chattering contact, the argument weakens. We therefore pre-register a contact-occupancy diagnostic: report the empirical fraction of probe time within \varepsilon of \mathcal S_i, and flag any pair whose fraction exceeds a threshold as lying outside the piecewise-Lipschitz regime.

