3. Capability Profiles as Scalar Marginals

3.1 Composition, not an undefined law

Let \rho_{\mathrm{probe}}\in\mathcal P(\Pi) be a distribution over policies. The task-conditioned capability marginal is
P^\tau_{ik}=\int_{\Pi}\mathrm{Law}_{\xi\sim P_{i,e,\pi}}\big(\varphi_k(\xi)\big)\,d\rho_{\mathrm{probe}}(\pi),\qquad e\sim\mu_{\mathcal E}(\cdot\mid\tau). \tag{3}


Under A1–A2 this is a well-defined probability measure on \mathbb R. The probe distribution is a design choice, and sensitivity to it is pre-registered.

3.2 Theorem 1 (marginalisation is necessary, not convenient)

Theorem 1 (curse avoidance). Let P\in\mathcal P(\mathbb R^d) with d\ge3 and finite second moment. Then \mathbb E\,W_1(\hat P_n,P)=\Omega(n^{-1/d}). Wasserstein distances suffer from the curse of dimensionality, with sample complexity growing exponentially in dimension ; the n^{-1/2} rate is a one-dimensional phenomenon. Consequently, if capability profiles were represented as joint d-dimensional distributions with d\ge3, the probe sample size required for tolerance \eta_D would scale as \eta_D^{-d}, making the predictive-economy condition unattainable.

Architectural implication. The capability profile must be a finite family of scalar marginals. This is forced by the optimal-transport rate, not chosen for convenience.

3.3 Lemma 1 (directional decomposition) and Lemma 2 (no triangle inequality)

Lemma 1. For scalar distributions P,Q with quantile functions Q_P,Q_Q,
W_1(P,Q)=\int_0^1\big(Q_P-Q_Q\big)_+\,dq+\int_0^1\big(Q_Q-Q_P\big)_+\,dq=D^{\to}(P\to Q)+D^{\leftarrow}(P\to Q). \tag{4}

Lemma 2. D^{\to} does not satisfy the triangle inequality in general. A strict violation appears with P=\mathrm{Unif}[0,2], Q=\mathrm{Unif}[1,3], R=\mathrm{Unif}[2,4]: D^{\to}(P\to Q)=0.5, D^{\to}(Q\to R)=0.5, but D^{\to}(P\to R)=1.5>1.

Consequence. D_C^{\to} and D_C^{\leftarrow} enter the predictive models as features, not as distances in a metric regression.

