4. Theory: Quantitative and Operational

4.1 Proposition 1′ (quantitative rank reversal)

Define the pairwise reversal rate R_{\mathcal L,\mathcal L'} as the probability over body triples that the sign of the transfer-cost ordering under \mathcal L differs from that under \mathcal L'. If R_{\mathcal L,\mathcal L'}>0, no single scalar body-only statistic is ordinally faithful to both learners with probability greater than 1-R_{\mathcal L,\mathcal L'}. The learner-indexed formulation is therefore necessary on the reversal event, and H3 estimates R_{\mathcal L,\mathcal L'} with a family-clustered bootstrap interval against a pre-registered R_{\min}=0.10.

4.2 Theorem 2 (simulation lemma with reward mismatch and visited-distribution mismatch)

Let \psi_{ij}:\mathcal X_i\to\mathcal X_j be a measurable bijection with measurable inverse, and let \epsilon_R=\sup_{x,u}|r_i(x,u)-r_j(\psi_{ij}x,\psi_{ij}u)|<\infty. Then
\big|V_i^\pi(x)-V_j^{\pi\circ\psi_{ij}}(\psi_{ij}x)\big|
\le \frac{\gamma R_{\max}}{(1-\gamma)^2}\,\delta_{ij}(\rho_\pi)+\frac{\epsilon_R}{1-\gamma}. \tag{5}

The first term uses the standard simulation-lemma argument with (I-\gamma P_j^\pi)^{-1} bounded in sup-norm by (1-\gamma)^{-1}, but the transition gap is the visited-distribution expectation \delta_{ij}(\rho_\pi), not a worst-case supremum. This is the object a capability profile can plausibly control, because the probe samples closed-loop trajectories and therefore samples \rho_\pi.

4.3 Theorem 3 (capability controls dynamics mismatch)

Suppose the task-conditioned profile includes a transition-sensitive component k^\dagger whose functional is Lipschitz in the one-step predictive distribution:
\big|\varphi_{k^\dagger}(x,u)-\varphi_{k^\dagger}(\psi x,\psi u)\big|\le L_\varphi\,\mathrm{TV}\big(F_i(\cdot\mid x,u),\psi^*F_j(\cdot\mid \psi x,\psi u)\big). \tag{6}


Then on the support of the probe-induced occupancy,
\delta_{ij}(\rho_\pi)\le \frac{1}{L_\varphi}\Big(D^{\leftarrow}_{k^\dagger}(i\to j)+D^{\to}_{k^\dagger}(i\to j)\Big)+o(1). \tag{7}

This is the theorem that makes UEA predictive rather than heuristic. Its premise is empirical: a transition-sensitive capability component must exist and be probe-estimable. Theorem 3′ operationalises the search.

4.4 Theorem 3′ (operational selection rule)

Let
k^\dagger=\arg\max_{k\in\mathcal K_{\mathrm{primary}}}\ \widehat{\mathrm{Spearman}}\big(D^{\bullet}_k(i\to j),\ \hat\delta_{ij}\big), \tag{8}


where \hat\delta_{ij} is the empirical TV mismatch on probe trajectories. Strong rank association between a Lipschitz-type quantity and an error metric is documented in the uncertainty-estimation literature, with Spearman coefficients above 0.84 reported between local Lipschitz values and mean absolute error . The selection rule is novel as an identification procedure, but it is grounded in this documented monotone relationship.

Operational bridge condition. If \widehat{\mathrm{Spearman}}(D^{\bullet}_{k^\dagger},\hat\delta_{ij})\ge\rho_{\min} (proposal \rho_{\min}=0.3) with a family-clustered bootstrap lower bound above \rho_{\min}, then Theorem 3 applies with k^\dagger. Otherwise K1 triggers: no transition-sensitive capability component was identified, and the capability-distance claim is abandoned. The correlation threshold is a falsification test for the candidate, not a proof of Lipschitz continuity.

4.5 Theorem 4 (discordant-design necessity)

Let \mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}} be the cell where D_M lies in its lowest tercile and D_C in its highest, and let \pi(\mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}})=p_{\mathrm{disc}}>0. For any predictor g(D_M),
\mathbb E\big[(\log N^\star-g(D_M))^2\big]\ \ge\ p_{\mathrm{disc}}\cdot\mathrm{Var}\big(\log N^\star\mid \mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}}\big). \tag{9}


The error on the discordant cell is at least the conditional variance, and g cannot reduce it because it does not observe D_C. The decorrelated 3\times3 design is therefore necessary to make the capability-distance test informative, not merely elegant.

4.6 Corollary 5 (probe-economy certificate)

Under A4 with J(P)\le J_{\max}, achieving \mathbb E|\hat D_C-D_C|\le\eta_D requires
n_p\ \ge\ \Big(\frac{J_{\max}}{\eta_D}\Big)^2. \tag{10}


Hence the predictive-economy condition holds only if
\varrho=\frac{(J_{\max}/\eta_D)^2\,T_{\mathrm{probe}}}{\mathbb E[N^\star]}\ \le\ \varrho_{\max}. \tag{11}


This is an a priori check on A3 before running the experiment: estimate J_{\max} from a pilot and plug in.

4.7 Lemma 6 (feasibility-margin stability)

The feasibility margin is defined, with l_h the Lipschitz constant of the barrier function h, as c_f=\gamma_2-(\gamma_1+2l_h w_{x,\max}), and robust safety is guaranteed whenever c_f>0 . In the UEA notation, define
\mu_i=\inf_{x\in\mathcal C_i}\ \max_{u\in\mathcal U_i}\Big[L_{f_i}h_i+L_{g_i}h_i\,u+\alpha(h_i)-\|\nabla h_i\|\delta_i\Big]. \tag{12}


Suppose h_i is L_h-Lipschitz on \mathcal C_i and the dynamics perturbation satisfies \|f_i-f_j\|_\infty+\|g_i-g_j\|_\infty+|\delta_i-\delta_j|\le\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}. Then
\mu_j\ \ge\ \mu_i-L_h\,\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}-o(\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}). \tag{13}


Consequently, if \mu_i>L_h\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}, then \mu_j>0 and the CBF guarantee transfers. The verification cost V_{i\to j} is therefore a predictable function of dynamics distance, and the runtime-assurance layer is not a separate story but part of the transfer-cost accounting.

