5. Statistical Model: Censored Log-Normal AFT with Crossed Random Effects

5.1 Model specification and censored ELPD

For a pair (i,j), learner \mathcal L_\ell, and task \tau:
\log N^\star_{i\to j\ell\tau}=\mu(\mathbf x_{ij},\mathcal L_\ell,\tau)+u_i+v_j+\varepsilon_{ij\ell\tau},\qquad \varepsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2), \tag{14}


with u_i\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma_u^2), v_j\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma_v^2), and right-censoring at N_{\max}. Log-normal AFT models with random effects are established for correlated survival data ; with Gaussian \varepsilon, the model is a linear mixed model with Tobit-type censoring, which is the most tractable route.

The primary error distribution is \mathcal N; Weibull is a robustness check. The out-of-fold predictive likelihood is the censored ELPD:
\mathrm{elpd}_i=d_i\log f(t_i\mid\theta)+(1-d_i)\log S(t_i\mid\theta), \tag{15}


and model comparison uses PSIS-LOO or family-clustered K-fold with this censored pointwise term . Computing \Lambda on an uncensored density would be meaningless.

5.2 Identifiability caveat and degree diagnostic

Crossed random effects identifiability is a declared limitation, not a solved problem: data conditions for sufficiently identifying group effects remain an open question, especially when each group has few repeated measurements . With ~450 pairs over ~300 bodies, the average degree is low. We therefore (i) pre-register a degree diagnostic, reporting the distributions of source and target degrees; (ii) use Bayesian estimation with weakly informative priors on \sigma_u,\sigma_v as the primary route, since priors regularise variance components where MLE may not; (iii) declare the model weakly identified if the median degree is below 5, and report fixed-effects-only robustness models in that case; (iv) report a sensitivity analysis over prior scale.

5.3 Conformal intervals for N^\star

The AFT prediction interval is replaced by a censoring-adjusted conformal interval. Conformalized survival analysis produces calibrated, covariate-dependent lower predictive bounds under Type-I right-censoring with exogenous censoring times, and satisfies a doubly robust coverage property under conditionally independent censoring when either the censoring mechanism or the conditional survival function is estimated well . A naïve split-conformal that treats censored observations as exact will under-cover, so the calibration procedure must explicitly handle censoring. The conformal interval is a secondary endpoint; the point prediction remains primary.

5.4 Separation Leakage Index with explicit nesting

The nested models are M_0\subset M_2\subset M_3: M_0 contains learner and task effects, M_2 adds D_C^{\to},D_C^{\leftarrow}, M_3 adds D_M. Nesting is verified structurally and empirically. The Separation Leakage Index is
\Lambda=\frac{L_3-L_2}{L_3-L_0}\in[0,1], \tag{16}


and is reported only if L_3-L_0 is significantly positive on held-out families; otherwise \Lambda is declared unidentified, not reported as a ratio. Paired DeLong tests on the censored concordance index accompany the ELPD differences.

5.5 Power analysis

For a 3\times3 stratification detecting \Delta L_{21}=0.10 nats with 80% power at \alpha=0.05 two-sided and family-clustered inference, approximately n_{\mathrm{cell}}\approx 50 pairs per cell are required. With design effect \mathrm{DEFF}=1+(m-1)\rho_{\mathrm{ICC}}, m\approx1.5 pairs per body, and a conservative \rho_{\mathrm{ICC}}=0.4, \mathrm{DEFF}=1.2, giving n_{\mathrm{cell}}\approx 60 and N_{\mathrm{pairs}}\approx 540. This is the basis for the 300–500 body population: pairs, not bodies, are the unit of analysis and must be powered accordingly.

