6. Architecture and Design Patterns

6.1 Layer stack

```text
L4  Research & pre-registration layer
    hypothesis registry · sealed evaluation · power audit
L3  Predictive layer (learner-indexed)
    TransferCostPredictor · CapabilityDistance · AFT model
L2  Adaptation layer
    LearnerAdapter (RL / VLA) · ProbeProtocol
L1  Runtime assurance layer
    ISSf-CBF filter · feasibility-margin monitor
L0  Embodiment abstraction layer
    UBI descriptor · URDF/SDF adapter · ROS 2 bridge
```

The independence of L1 from L2 is architectural: the safety filter consumes u_{\mathrm{nom}} and returns u_{\mathrm{safe}}, with no access to the learner’s internal state. This is what makes V_{i\to j} measurable as a separate cost.

6.2 UBI descriptor

The Universal Body Interface record is
\mathcal D_i=\big(\sigma_i,\ \hat{\mathbf c}_i,\ \Sigma_i,\ \Gamma_i,\ h_i,\ \kappa_i,\ J\text{-certificate}_i\big), \tag{17}


where the J-certificate is the empirical estimate of J(\hat P_{ik}) certifying A4. It is a descriptor above URDF/SDF and ROS 2, not a replacement for them.

6.3 Design patterns

Pattern Where Purpose
Adapter L0 UBI ↔ URDF/SDF/ROS 2 Translate existing descriptions into the capability record.
Strategy L2 LearnerAdapter Swap RL vs VLA learners without changing L3; learner-indexing is structural.
Template Method L2 ProbeProtocol Fix the rollout skeleton; subclasses specify probe behaviours.
Observer L1 feasibility monitor Subscribe to \mu_i; emit a qualitative transfer-cost event if \mu_i crosses below zero.
Proxy L3 compute placement Enforce the latency/bandwidth feasibility condition before a policy acts.
Specification L4 hypothesis registry Encapsulate pre-registered thresholds as immutable objects; deviations are logged.

6.4 Core algorithms

Algorithm 1 — ProbeEstimation(B_i,\rho_{\mathrm{probe}},n_p). Sample n_p policies from \rho_{\mathrm{probe}}, execute closed-loop rollouts, record \varphi_k, form empirical marginals, compute J(\hat P_{ik}) to certify A4, and return the marginals with the probe cost C_{\mathrm{probe}}.

Algorithm 2 — TransferCostPredict(B_i,B_j,\mathcal L,\tau). Compute D_C^{\to},D_C^{\leftarrow} from the estimated marginals; evaluate the AFT linear predictor with crossed effects frozen at training estimates; return \widehat{\log N^\star} with a censoring-adjusted conformal interval and a discordant-cell support flag.

Algorithm 3 — RuntimeAssuranceFilter(x,u_{\mathrm{nom}},h_i,f_i,g_i,\delta_i). Evaluate the ISSf-CBF constraint; if feasible, solve the QP and update the running \mu_i estimate; if infeasible, emit a violation event and fall back to a pre-registered safe action. The event is counted in V_{i\to j}.

Algorithm 4 — VerificationCostDecomposition(B_j,h_j). Decompose V_{i\to j} into model-identification cost to reach a pre-registered \delta_j bound, feasibility-margin cost to achieve \mu_j>0, and filter-calibration cost. A filter feasible only after extensive identification is a different engineering object from one feasible out of the box.

