7. Hypotheses, Kill Criteria and Pre-Registered Thresholds

H1 (predictive value). \Delta L_{21}=L_2-L_1>m_1 on held-out families, with family-clustered bootstrap lower bound above zero.

H2 (partial separability). L_3-L_2>m_2 and 0<\Lambda<1.

H3 (learner dependence). The learner \times distance interaction improves held-out likelihood, and R_{\mathcal L,\mathcal L'} exceeds R_{\min}.

H4 (predict-before-transfer). \varrho\le\varrho_{\max} and \Delta L_{\mathrm{probe}}\le\eta.

H5 (runtime assurance). Success degradation \le5 percentage points; zero hard-constraint violations on the evaluated suite; added latency \le10\% of the control-cycle budget.

Threshold Value Role
m_1 0.10 nats H1 minimum signal
m_2 0.05 nats H2 minimum signal
\varrho_{\max} 0.10 A3 predictive economy
\eta 0.05 nats probe-vs-oracle gap
R_{\min} 0.10 learner non-universality
\rho_{\min} 0.30 transition-sensitive component selection

Kill criteria. K1: \Delta L_{21}\le0 with CI including zero \Rightarrow capability-distance novelty abandoned. K2: \varrho>0.10 or \Delta L_{\mathrm{probe}}>0.05 \Rightarrow A3 fails. K3: R_{\mathcal L,\mathcal L'} lower bound <0.10 \Rightarrow learner-indexed formulation weakened to a robustness remark. K4: H5 thresholds fail \Rightarrow safety-integration claim withdrawn. K5: sign of \Delta L_{21} inconsistent across the two simulators in the majority of held-out families \Rightarrow conclusions restricted to the simulator where it holds.

---

8. Threats to Validity

The simulation gap is a first-class threat: held-out families mitigate overfitting to the generator’s design space but do not test the design space of real robots. Cross-simulator replication is therefore a minimum validity condition, not a robustness check. The contact-occupancy diagnostic limits the piecewise-Lipschitz argument to pairs that do not chatter. The crossed-effects model is weakly identified at low degree and is reported as such. The conformal guarantee is finite-sample under Type-I censoring and doubly robust under conditionally independent censoring; general right-censoring is a declared limitation. The J-certificate is an empirical estimate and may itself be noisy. Nothing here addresses identity, experience or consciousness transfer.

---

9. Conclusion

UEA is a falsifiable theory, not a new robot architecture. Its central sentence is:

Task-conditioned scalar capability marginals, estimated by a cheap probe, control the visited-distribution dynamics mismatch between bodies, and therefore predict censored adaptation sample complexity better than morphology — with the residual dependence quantified by a leakage index whose identifiability is itself pre-registered.

Theorem 3 supplies the conditional bridge; Theorem 3′ supplies the operational selection rule and its kill criterion; Theorem 4 supplies the lower bound that makes the decorrelated design necessary; Corollary 5 supplies the a priori economy certificate; Lemma 6 makes runtime-assurance calibration a predictable transfer cost; and the censored statistical model makes every comparison honest under right-censoring. If K1 triggers, the bet is lost and the result is still a publishable negative finding about what determines transfer cost. The body should be replaceable; the evidence for that should not be assumed.


APPENDICES:

Appendix A — Measure-Theoretic Foundations

A.1 Why Polish spaces and Borel kernels are required

The main text asserts that the closed-loop law P_{i,e,\pi} exists. That assertion is not free. The Ionescu–Tulcea theorem guarantees the existence and uniqueness of a probability measure on the product space (\mathcal X_i\times\mathcal U_i)^{\mathbb N} generated by a sequence of Borel-measurable Markov kernels, provided the underlying spaces are standard Borel. Polish spaces are standard Borel, and the Borel \sigma-algebra on a Polish space is countably generated. Without this structure, the phrase “the distribution of the closed-loop trajectory” has no referent, and every subsequent expectation—including P^\tau_{ik} in (3)—is undefined.

Definition A.1 (Embodiment as a measurable dynamical system). An embodiment is a tuple

B_i=(\mathcal X_i,\mathcal U_i,\mathcal Z_i,F_i,H_i,\Gamma_i,\kappa_i,\mathcal S_i),

where \mathcal X_i,\mathcal U_i,\mathcal Z_i are Polish; F_i:\mathcal X_i\times\mathcal U_i\times\mathcal E\to\mathcal P(\mathcal X_i) and H_i:\mathcal X_i\times\mathcal E\to\mathcal P(\mathcal Z_i) are Borel-measurable Markov kernels; \Gamma_i\subseteq\mathcal X_i\times\mathcal U_i is closed; \kappa_i is the compute placement with its latency/bandwidth constraints; and \mathcal S_i\subset\mathcal X_i\times\mathcal U_i is the contact set of Assumption A3.

Lemma A.1 (closed-loop law). Under A0–A1, for every e\in\mathcal E and every measurable policy \pi:\mathcal Z_i^*\to\mathcal P(\mathcal U_i), there exists a unique probability measure P_{i,e,\pi}\in\mathcal P((\mathcal X_i\times\mathcal U_i)^{\mathbb N}) whose finite-dimensional marginals are consistent with F_i,H_i,\pi.

Proof. This is a direct application of the Ionescu–Tulcea theorem. The kernels are Borel-measurable by A1, the spaces are standard Borel by A0, and the policy is measurable by definition. ∎

A.2 The capability marginal as a well-defined object

The main text defines

P^\tau_{ik}=\int_{\Pi}\mathrm{Law}_{\xi\sim P_{i,e,\pi}}\big(\varphi_k(\xi)\big)\,d\rho_{\mathrm{probe}}(\pi).

For this to be a probability measure on \mathbb R, the map \pi\mapsto \mathrm{Law}(\varphi_k(\xi)) must be measurable with respect to the Borel \sigma-algebra on \mathcal P(\mathbb R), and \varphi_k must be bounded and measurable by A2.

Lemma A.2. Under A1–A2, the map \pi\mapsto \mathrm{Law}_{\xi\sim P_{i,e,\pi}}(\varphi_k(\xi)) is Borel-measurable, and P^\tau_{ik} is a well-defined probability measure on \mathbb R with finite moments of all orders.

Proof sketch. The closed-loop law depends measurably on \pi by A1; composition with the bounded measurable \varphi_k preserves measurability; the Bochner integral over \Pi is therefore well defined. Boundedness of \varphi_k gives finite moments. ∎

