Appendix B — Proofs for the Optimal-Transport Lemmas

B.1 Lemma 1 (directional decomposition)

Lemma 1. For scalar distributions P,Q on \mathbb R with quantile functions Q_P,Q_Q,

W_1(P,Q)=\int_0^1(Q_P-Q_Q)_+\,dq+\int_0^1(Q_Q-Q_P)_+\,dq.

Proof. In one dimension, the optimal coupling for W_1 is the monotone (quantile) coupling, and

W_1(P,Q)=\int_0^1|Q_P(q)-Q_Q(q)|\,dq.

The identity |a-b|=(a-b)_+ +(b-a)_+ gives the result. ∎

Corollary B.1. D^{\to}(P\to Q) is the expected positive part of the quantile difference; D^{\leftarrow} is the expected negative part. Their sum is W_1(P,Q), which is symmetric; the asymmetry of D^{\to} comes from the positive part.

B.2 Lemma 2 (failure of the triangle inequality)

Lemma 2. D^{\to} does not satisfy the triangle inequality in general.

Proof. Take P=\mathrm{Unif}[0,2], Q=\mathrm{Unif}[1,3], R=\mathrm{Unif}[2,4]. Direct computation of the quantile functions gives D^{\to}(P\to Q)=0.5, D^{\to}(Q\to R)=0.5, and D^{\to}(P\to R)=1.5. Since 1.5>0.5+0.5, the inequality fails. ∎

Architectural consequence. D^{\to} and D^{\leftarrow} are features in a regression, not distances in a metric space. The AFT model in Appendix E does not require the triangle inequality; it requires only that the features are measurable and finite, which they are under A2 and A4.

B.3 Theorem 1 (curse avoidance)

Theorem 1. Let P\in\mathcal P(\mathbb R^d) with d\ge3 and finite second moment. Then \mathbb E\,W_1(\hat P_n,P)=\Omega(n^{-1/d}).

Proof. The sharp rate for the empirical measure in W_1 under a finite second moment is n^{-1/d} for d\ge3; the n^{-1/2} rate is a one-dimensional phenomenon. The lower bound is the matching half of the Fournier–Guillin theorem. ∎

Corollary B.2 (marginalisation is forced). If capability profiles were represented as joint d-dimensional distributions with d\ge3, the probe sample size required for \mathbb E W_1(\hat P_n,P)\le\eta_D would scale as \eta_D^{-d}. For any \eta_D<1 and d\ge3, this is super-linear in the tolerance and makes the economy condition A3 unattainable for any realistic probe budget.

