Then, on the support of the probe-induced occupancy,

\delta_{ij}(\rho_\pi)\le \frac{1}{L_\varphi}\big(D^{\leftarrow}_{k^\dagger}(i\to j)+D^{\to}_{k^\dagger}(i\to j)\big)+o(1).

Proof sketch. The Lipschitz condition gives \mathrm{TV}\le L_\varphi^{-1}|\Delta\varphi_{k^\dagger}|. Taking expectations over \rho_\pi and decomposing the absolute value into positive and negative parts yields the directional distances of Lemma 1. The o(1) term vanishes as the probe distribution becomes dense in the policy class, so that the probe-induced occupancy converges to \rho_\pi. ∎

Remark C.1. The theorem is conditional on the existence of k^\dagger. It does not assert that such a component exists for every embodiment pair. That is an empirical question, and Theorem 3′ is the procedure for answering it.

C.3 Theorem 3′ (operational selection rule)

The selection rule is

k^\dagger=\arg\max_{k\in\mathcal K_{\mathrm{primary}}}\widehat{\mathrm{Spearman}}\big(D^{\bullet}_k(i\to j),\hat\delta_{ij}\big).

The Spearman coefficient is a rank-based measure that is well defined for any two finite samples with no ties (ties broken by average ranks). The operational bridge condition is: if the family-clustered bootstrap lower bound of \widehat{\mathrm{Spearman}}(D^{\bullet}_{k^\dagger},\hat\delta_{ij}) exceeds \rho_{\min}=0.3, then Theorem 3 applies with k^\dagger; otherwise K1 triggers.

Justification for the threshold. The value \rho_{\min}=0.3 is an engineering choice, not a derived constant. It is chosen so that the selected component explains at least a moderate rank association with the empirical mismatch; a lower threshold would admit components with negligible predictive content, while a higher threshold would risk failing to identify any component even when the bridge holds. The threshold is pre-registered and immovable.

C.4 Theorem 4 (discordant-design necessity)

Theorem 4. Let \mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}} be the cell where D_M is in its lowest tercile and D_C in its highest, with \pi(\mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}})=p_{\mathrm{disc}}>0. For any predictor g(D_M),

\mathbb E\big[(\log N^\star-g(D_M))^2\big]\ge p_{\mathrm{disc}}\,\mathrm{Var}\big(\log N^\star\mid\mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}}\big).

Proof. Decompose the expectation over the partition \{\mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}},\mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}}^c\}. On \mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}}, g(D_M) is a function of a variable that is conditionally independent of D_C given the cell definition; the best constant predictor is the conditional mean, and the residual variance is the conditional variance. The contribution from \mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}} is therefore p_{\mathrm{disc}} times that conditional variance. The full expectation is at least this contribution. ∎

Corollary C.2. If p_{\mathrm{disc}}>0 and \mathrm{Var}(\log N^\star\mid\mathcal P_{\mathrm{disc}})>0, then no morphology-only predictor can achieve zero excess risk on the discordant cell. The 3\times3 design is necessary to expose this irreducible error.

C.5 Corollary 5 (probe-economy certificate)

Corollary 5. Under A4 with J(P)\le J_{\max}, achieving \mathbb E|\hat D_C-D_C|\le\eta_D requires

n_p\ge\Big(\frac{J_{\max}}{\eta_D}\Big)^2.

Hence A3 holds only if

\varrho=\frac{(J_{\max}/\eta_D)^2\,T_{\mathrm{probe}}}{\mathbb E[N^\star]}\le\varrho_{\max}.

Proof. By Lemma 1 and the n^{-1/2} rate in one dimension, the expected error in the directional distance is bounded by \sum_k w_k(J_{ik}+J_{jk})/\sqrt{n_p}. Setting this equal to \eta_D and bounding the entropic integrals by J_{\max} gives the sample size. The economy ratio follows by multiplying the probe cost per sample. ∎

C.6 Lemma 6 (feasibility-margin stability)

Lemma 6. Let h_i be L_h-Lipschitz on \mathcal C_i, and suppose

\|f_i-f_j\|_\infty+\|g_i-g_j\|_\infty+|\delta_i-\delta_j|\le\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}.

Then

\mu_j\ge\mu_i-L_h\,\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}-o(\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}).

Consequently, if \mu_i>L_h\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}, then \mu_j>0 and the CBF guarantee transfers.

Proof. The feasibility margin \mu_i is the infimum over x\in\mathcal C_i of the maximum over u of the ISSf-CBF constraint. The constraint is Lipschitz in (f_i,g_i,\delta_i,h_i) with constant proportional to L_h, because the Lie derivatives are linear in f_i,g_i, the disturbance term is \|\nabla h_i\|\delta_i, and h_i itself is L_h-Lipschitz. Hence perturbing the dynamics by \epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}} perturbs the margin by at most L_h\epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}} to first order. The ISSf-CBF robustness margin \varrho(h,\gamma) is monotone in the disturbance bound, which gives the stated inequality. ∎

Remark C.3. The lemma makes the verification cost V_{i\to j} a function of \epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}: to achieve \mu_j>0, it suffices that \epsilon_{\mathrm{dyn}}<\mu_i/L_h. The cost of reaching that bound is the model-identification cost, which is one of the three components of V_{i\to j} in Algorithm 4.

