Appendix D — Statistical Derivations

D.1 Censored ELPD

For right-censored observations (t_i,d_i), where d_i=1 if the event is observed and d_i=0 if censored at t_i, the pointwise log predictive density is

\mathrm{lpd}_i=\begin{cases}\log f(t_i\mid\theta),&d_i=1,\\[2pt]\log S(t_i\mid\theta),&d_i=0.\end{cases}

Equivalently, \mathrm{lpd}_i=d_i\log f(t_i\mid\theta)+(1-d_i)\log S(t_i\mid\theta). LOO and WAIC computed from this likelihood are appropriate for survival models with right-censoring. The expected log predictive density is the sum of the pointwise terms over held-out observations; PSIS-LOO or family-clustered K-fold provides the out-of-fold estimate.

D.2 Conformal survival calibration

The conformal lower predictive bound for a survival time is constructed by wrapping any survival regression algorithm. In the Type I right-censoring setting, the bound has finite-sample marginal coverage without assumptions beyond exchangeability. Under conditionally independent censoring, the bound satisfies a doubly robust property: marginal coverage is approximately guaranteed if either the censoring mechanism or the conditional survival function is estimated well. A naïve split-conformal that treats censored observations as exact events will under-cover because the censored residual distribution is stochastically larger than the uncensored one. The calibration procedure must therefore either (i) restrict to the Type I setting with exogenous censoring times, or (ii) use the doubly robust weighting of Candès–Lei–Ren.

D.3 Crossed random effects: identifiability as a declared limitation

The model

\log N^\star_{i\to j}=\mu(\mathbf x_{ij})+u_i+v_j+\varepsilon_{ij},\qquad u_i\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma_u^2),\;v_j\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma_v^2),

is a linear mixed model with crossed random effects and right-censoring. Identifiability of (\sigma_u^2,\sigma_v^2) requires sufficient repeated measurements per body. With ~450 pairs over ~300 bodies, the average degree is ~3; this is low. We therefore do not claim MLE consistency for the variance components. The primary estimation route is Bayesian with weakly informative priors on \sigma_u,\sigma_v, which regularise the variance components where the likelihood is flat. The degree diagnostic is pre-registered: if the median source or target degree is below 5, the crossed-effects model is declared weakly identified and fixed-effects-only models are reported alongside.

D.4 Power analysis

For a 3\times3 stratification with n_{\mathrm{cell}} pairs per cell, detecting a difference \Delta L_{21}=0.10 nats at \alpha=0.05 (two-sided) with power 0.80 requires approximately n_{\mathrm{cell}}=50 under independent pairs. With design effect \mathrm{DEFF}=1+(m-1)\rho_{\mathrm{ICC}}, where m\approx1.5 pairs per body and \rho_{\mathrm{ICC}}=0.4, \mathrm{DEFF}=1.2, giving n_{\mathrm{cell}}\approx60 and N_{\mathrm{pairs}}\approx540. The 300–500 body population provides sufficient pairs at this design effect provided that the pair graph is connected and the discordant cells are populated above chance.

